---
layout: base.njk
title: "Inflowwww vs. Infloww: a comparison you can check, including where we lose"
description: "Inflowwww vs. Infloww, row by row: price, platforms, support, team logins and unbuilt screens, including where we lose."
---
{#- Follows infloww.com/infloww-vs-onlymonster: hero, claim blocks, a cost section, a feature table.
    Every row is checkable: their words plus where we read them, our route or README line. No claims about
    Infloww's security or bans. Below `sm` the table renders as
    stacked cards (theirs first, like the table) from the same rows, so the Inflowwww column never scrolls off a phone. -#}
{%- set captured = "captured " ~ site.captureDate -%}
{%- set stamped = "their page stamped Oct 10, 2026" -%}
{%- set rows = [
  { topic: "Price",
    theirs: '"Starting at just $40" per creator profile per month, up to $500 for a creator earning over $75,000.',
    theirsSource: "infloww.com/pricing, " ~ captured,
    ours: "$0. The software is MIT licensed. You pay for the server you run it on.",
    oursSource: "LICENSE, README quick start" },
  { topic: "What the price depends on",
    theirs: '"Monthly pricing per creator profile", set by the "Creator\'s monthly earnings" slider.',
    theirsSource: "infloww.com/pricing, " ~ captured,
    ours: "Nothing. Our slider has one position.",
    oursSource: "README" },
  { topic: "Platforms",
    theirs: '"Manage creators across OnlyFans, Fansly, Fanvue and MYM."',
    theirsSource: "infloww.com meta description, " ~ captured,
    ours: "OnlyFans. Fansly is partly built: the Docker image (a packaged copy of the app) ships the Fansly client, and 4 of the Fansly Manager's 16 pages are built. No Fanvue, no MYM.",
    oursSource: "README: Repository layout (fansly/)",
    lose: true },
  { topic: "Uptime",
    theirs: '99.99% "in the last 90 days" on the homepage; 99.96% on the comparison pages.',
    theirsSource: "infloww.com, " ~ captured ~ "; infloww.com/infloww-vs-onlymonster, " ~ stamped,
    ours: "No figure and no SLA. An autoheal container restarts unhealthy services; the server itself is up to your host.",
    oursSource: "README (openinflowwww-autoheal)",
    lose: true },
  { topic: "Support",
    theirs: '"24/7 technical support" on every plan.',
    theirsSource: "infloww.com/pricing, " ~ captured,
    ours: "No support desk and no SLA. The README, the code and GitHub issues.",
    oursSource: "README",
    lose: true },
  { topic: "Where it runs",
    theirs: 'A desktop app: Mac .dmg files for Apple Silicon and Intel, plus a "Looking for the Windows download?" toggle.',
    theirsSource: "infloww.com/download, " ~ stamped,
    ours: "Your server, with Docker Compose. A web app in any browser, plus an Electron desktop shell that loads it.",
    oursSource: "README: Quick start, Surfaces" },
  { topic: "Phones",
    theirs: 'A "Mobile accessible Dashboard" is on their roadmap, in progress.',
    theirsSource: "infloww.com/product-roadmap, " ~ stamped,
    ours: "A responsive web app today. There is no native phone app.",
    oursSource: "README: Surfaces; docs/routes.md: Responsive behaviour" },
  { topic: "Where the data lives",
    theirs: 'On their service. Their privacy policy makes Infinni a processor for "Customer Data".',
    theirsSource: "infloww.com/privacy-policy, last updated Aug 17, 2026",
    ours: "On your server: a SQLite file and a sessions folder, bind-mounted from the host.",
    oursSource: "README quick start; docker-compose.yml" },
  { topic: "Chat inbox",
    theirs: "Messages Pro™",
    theirsSource: "infloww.com, " ~ captured,
    ours: "Messages Pro: chat list, thread and a fan insights rail with fan notes.",
    oursSource: "/messages-pro" },
  { topic: "Team logins",
    theirs: '"Roles and permissions", listed on their Fansly, mym and Fanvue cards.',
    theirsSource: "infloww.com/pricing, " ~ captured,
    ours: "Partly built. Per-user logins: the owner at /login, each chatter at /chatter-login from a one-time invite link, plus a share token for the dashboard link. The share token is not the lock: sign-up at /login is open to anyone who can reach the server, and dashboard passwords are stored in plain text; see Help > Security notes. Two fixed roles, owner and chatter; no custom roles. Each chatter gets the accounts you grant and the vault folders you pick (a new chatter sees the whole vault until you pick folders), and owner pages such as Creators and Vault Pro are hidden from them.",
    oursSource: "/login, /chatter-login, /employees" },
  { topic: "Mass messages",
    theirs: "Smart Messages™, Priority mass messages",
    theirsSource: "infloww.com, " ~ captured,
    ours: "Smart Messages, with mass and scheduled sends.",
    oursSource: "/growth/smart-messages" },
  { topic: "Lists",
    theirs: "Smart lists",
    theirsSource: "infloww.com, " ~ captured,
    ours: "Smart lists.",
    oursSource: "/growth/smart-lists" },
  { topic: "Vault",
    theirs: "Vault Pro™",
    theirsSource: "infloww.com, " ~ captured,
    ours: "Vault Pro, sorted by the owner. Chatters attach media from the chat composer, limited to the folders the owner grants; a new chatter sees the whole vault until the owner picks folders.",
    oursSource: "/growth/vault-pro" },
  { topic: "Reports",
    theirs: "Message dashboard, Fan reports, Employee reports",
    theirsSource: "infloww.com, " ~ captured,
    ours: "Creator, Employee and Fan reports, and the Message dashboard. Chatter logins put the chatter's name on every message; unclear sales wait in a Sales needing attribution card for the owner.",
    oursSource: "/analytics/*" },
  { topic: "Scripts",
    theirs: "Scripts",
    theirsSource: "infloww.com, " ~ captured,
    ours: "Scripts.",
    oursSource: "/growth/scripts" },
  { topic: "Proxies",
    theirs: "Unique proxies",
    theirsSource: "infloww.com, " ~ captured,
    ours: "Custom proxy: you bring your own proxies and assign them per creator.",
    oursSource: "/creators/proxy" },
  { topic: "Sensitive words",
    theirs: "Sensitive word blocker, listed on their OnlyFans card.",
    theirsSource: "infloww.com/pricing, " ~ captured,
    ours: "Partly built: the agency's Growth > Sensitive words list blocks matching sends from the mass, funnel and chat automations; the chat and mass composers only warn.",
    oursSource: "/growth/sensitive-words",
    lose: true },
  { topic: "Shift scheduling",
    theirs: "Employee shifts",
    theirsSource: "infloww.com, " ~ captured,
    ours: "Built: a week shift schedule, clock in and out, and clocked hours in the reports.",
    oursSource: "/employees/shifts" },
  { topic: "AI",
    theirs: '"Increase efficiency with our AI Copilot", tagged "In Beta".',
    theirsSource: "infloww.com, " ~ captured,
    ours: "Built: the composer's AI button suggests three replies to edit and send, on your own LLM key. The dashboard works without one.",
    oursSource: "/messages-pro" },
  { topic: "Screens we haven't built",
    theirs: "Not applicable. This row is about us.",
    theirsSource: "",
    ours: "Partly built: OF Manager (most pages show placeholder data) and the Fansly Manager (4 of 16 pages). Auto-renew campaigns are not built.",
    oursSource: "docs/routes.md",
    lose: true },
  { topic: "Getting started",
    theirs: '"Start free trial", "No credit card required", or "Book a demo".',
    theirsSource: "infloww.com, " ~ captured,
    ours: "Clone the repository, do the README's one-time setup (docker network create n8n_default, three state files and an .env), then run docker compose up -d --build. (The network name comes from the maintainer's own Traefik setup; you don't need n8n.) There is nothing to buy.",
    oursSource: "README quick start" }
] -%}

<section class="wrap pt-16 sm:pt-24">
  <p class="eyebrow">The $0 Infloww alternative</p>
  <h1 class="h-page mt-4 max-w-4xl">Inflowwww vs. Infloww: which one is right for you</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A row-by-row comparison you can check. Their column quotes their own site, with the date we read it. Our column
    points at a file or a route in our repository. Where we lose, the row says so.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
    <a class="btn-primary" href="{{ '/#deploy' | url }}">Deploy it yourself</a>
    <a class="btn-secondary" href="{{ '/switch-to-openinflowwww/' | url }}">How to switch</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="why" class="wrap mt-20">
  <h2 id="why" class="section-title">Why agencies look at Inflowwww</h2>
  <ul class="mt-8 grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-3">
    <li class="card">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The price doesn't move</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-muted">
        On their captured ladder, 40 creators cost between 40 x $40 = $1,600 and 40 x $500 = $20,000 a month,
        depending on what the creators earn. On ours, the software costs $0 for 40 creators; you still pay for the
        server and, if each creator gets its own IP, 40 proxies.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Your data stays on your server</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-muted">
        Sessions, proxies and the database are files on your server, next to the compose file. You can back them up,
        move them, or delete them. That makes guarding them your job: today the dashboard passwords in them are plain
        text (<a class="text-link" href="{{ '/roadmap/#known-gaps' | url }}">known gaps</a>).
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The code is the documentation</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-muted">
        Everything is in one repository under the MIT license. If a screen doesn't do what you need, you can
        read why, change it, and keep your change.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
  {% shot "creator-reports-combo.webp", "Inflowwww Creator reports on desktop and phone: one creator's weekly earnings by subscriptions, posts, messages and tips, with a daily chart", "Creator reports for a demo agency (synthetic data, 20 creators), read from the SQLite file on your server.", "mx-auto mt-12 max-w-3xl" %}
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="table" class="band-cream mt-20">
  <div class="wrap py-20 sm:py-28">
  <h2 id="table" class="section-title">Feature by feature</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Infloww facts come from infloww.com as captured on {{ site.captureDate }}, or from their pages stamped
    "Published Oct 10, 2026". Their product may have changed since.
  </p>
  {#- Phones: one card per row, theirs first like the table. The table below is hidden under `sm`, so nothing is read twice. -#}
  <ul class="mt-8 space-y-4 sm:hidden">
    {%- for row in rows %}
    <li class="rounded-2xl bg-white p-5 ring-1 ring-black/10">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">{{ row.topic }}</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm font-medium text-muted">Infloww, from their site</p>
      <p class="mt-1">
        {{ row.theirs }}
        {%- if row.theirsSource %}<span class="mt-1 block text-sm text-muted">{{ row.theirsSource }}</span>{% endif %}
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm font-medium text-muted">Inflowwww</p>
      <p class="mt-1">
        {%- if row.lose %}<span class="badge-parody mb-1">We lose this row</span><br />{% endif %}
        {{ row.ours }}
        <span class="mt-1 block text-sm text-muted">{{ row.oursSource }}</span>
      </p>
    </li>
    {%- endfor %}
  </ul>
  {#- No region wrapper: the section already carries the name, and the caption names the table. -#}
  <div class="mt-8 hidden overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl bg-white ring-1 ring-black/10 sm:block">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[36rem] text-left text-base">
      <caption class="sr-only">Feature by feature: Infloww's words and ours, one row per topic</caption>
      <thead class="bg-band">
        <tr>
          <th scope="col" class="w-44 px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Row</th>
          <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Infloww, from their site</th>
          <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Inflowwww</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {%- for row in rows %}
        <tr class="border-t border-line align-top">
          <th scope="row" class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold">{{ row.topic }}</th>
          <td class="px-4 py-4">
            {{ row.theirs }}
            {%- if row.theirsSource %}<span class="mt-1 block text-sm text-muted">{{ row.theirsSource }}</span>{% endif %}
          </td>
          <td class="px-4 py-4">
            {%- if row.lose %}<span class="badge-parody mb-1">We lose this row</span><br />{% endif %}
            {{ row.ours }}
            <span class="mt-1 block text-sm text-muted">{{ row.oursSource }}</span>
          </td>
        </tr>
        {%- endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="uptime" class="wrap mt-20">
  <div class="max-w-3xl">
  <h2 id="uptime" class="section-title">A note on the two uptime figures</h2>
  <div class="prose mt-6">
    <p>
      Infloww's homepage gives 99.99% uptime "in the last 90 days" (infloww.com, captured <span class="whitespace-nowrap">{{ site.captureDate }}</span>).
      Their comparison pages, infloww.com/infloww-vs-creatorhero and infloww.com/infloww-vs-onlymonster, give 99.96%
      ({{ stamped }}).
      We report both as published.
    </p>
    <p>We publish no uptime figure. Uptime is whatever your VPS does.</p>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="cost" class="wrap mt-20">
  <div class="max-w-3xl">
  <h2 id="cost" class="section-title">The true cost of downtime applies to us too</h2>
  <div class="prose mt-6">
    <p>
      Their comparison pages include a downtime calculator (infloww.com/infloww-vs-creatorhero, {{ stamped }}): at $550,000 of monthly portfolio revenue, a 3-hour outage
      costs $2,291.67. That is monthly revenue divided by 720 hours, times the hours down.
    </p>
    <p>
      The formula is fair, and it applies to any tool, including this one. If your server goes down, nobody at
      Inflowwww gets paged. Budget for a reliable host, keep backups, and know how to restart the stack before you
      need to.
    </p>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="stay" class="wrap mt-20 pb-20 sm:pb-28">
  <div class="max-w-3xl">
  <h2 id="stay" class="section-title">Who should stay on Infloww</h2>
  <div class="prose mt-6">
    <p>Plenty of agencies are better served by a hosted product. Stay on Infloww if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You want someone to call when something breaks, at any hour. They advertise 24/7 support; we have none.</li>
      <li>You need an uptime commitment from a vendor. We can't give one, because we don't run your server.</li>
      <li>You manage creators on Fansly, Fanvue or MYM. We support OnlyFans; Fansly is partly built.</li>
      <li>You rely on the OF Manager screens. Ours mostly show placeholder data.</li>
      <li>You don't want to run a server, apply updates, or own backups. Self-hosting makes those your job.</li>
      <li>Stay, or wait for the <a href="{{ '/roadmap/#known-gaps' | url }}">known gaps</a> to close, if nobody on your side can put the dashboard behind a VPN or a second login.</li>
      <li>Your team needs a native phone app. Neither of us offers one: their download page lists none, and their roadmap lists a mobile dashboard as in progress ({{ stamped }}). Ours is a responsive web app.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If none of those apply, and the per-creator bill does, read <a href="{{ '/switch-to-openinflowwww/' | url }}">how to switch</a>
      or the <a href="{{ '/enterprise/' | url }}">enterprise page</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>

